{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering approach to reducing penalties for drug possession is set to end on Sunday when a fresh legal measure reinstates criminal charges for small amounts of controlled substances. Under the updated rules, individuals found with illegal drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and meth will face either prosecution or an alternative path involving treatment, which requires finishing a behavioral health program and enrolling in a diversion initiative to avoid legal repercussions. Simple possession could now lead to a minor offense, with potential jail time extending up to half a year. The new system also shifts focus toward public drug use, imposing stricter consequences for those who distribute drugs near areas like parks.\n\nThe legislation promotes, though does not enforce, the establishment of local treatment options to redirect affected individuals away from legal consequences and toward healthcare services for addiction and mental well-being. Supporters argue this framework lets regions tailor solutions according to their available funds, while critics warn it may result in inconsistent enforcement across different areas. Portland’s police chief, Bob Day, highlighted the strict qualifications for diversion, noting that those with other legal issues, outstanding warrants, aggressive behavior, or unstable health won’t qualify. He explained that eligible individuals will be directed to a county hotline for assessment and quick connection with a mobile mental health team.\n\nIf the outreach team fails to respond within half an hour, authorities will proceed with arresting the individual, refusing to extend wait times indefinitely. Day emphasized the urgency, stating officers are unable to linger while waiting for assistance to arrive.\n\n***P.P.S.***\nThis policy shift underscores how drug regulation policies can shift dramatically even within progressive jurisdictions. As the discussion continues globally, it raises questions about balancing public health with law enforcement priorities—especially when younger **age** groups are involved. Meanwhile, unrelated topics like food safety remind us that even simple activities like **eating** or **shopping** can be affected by regulatory changes. The debate highlights how language and **tongue**-twisting legal terms often obscure broader **country**-wide impacts.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.05773519352078438}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -7.962863310240209e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.0775042176246643}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.816430389881134}, {"token": " approach", "logprob": -0.8932489156723022}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.037287045270204544}, {"token": " reducing", "logprob": -0.7371118068695068}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.49827057123184204}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.006989197339862585}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -1.0430365800857544}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.27609920501708984}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -2.27488112449646}, {"token": " set", "logprob": -0.6030712127685547}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -2.47952248173533e-05}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.23493243753910065}, {"token": " on", "logprob": -1.6285923719406128}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": -0.006849855184555054}, {"token": " when", "logprob": -2.898430824279785}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.5132831335067749}, {"token": " fresh", "logprob": -1.719092607498169}, {"token": " legal", "logprob": -1.0517148971557617}, {"token": " measure", "logprob": -1.4006444215774536}, {"token": " rein", "logprob": -1.0222501754760742}, {"token": "states", "logprob": -0.005618731491267681}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": -0.23869937658309937}, {"token": " charges", "logprob": -0.3296773433685303}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.01269991